WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
A secretory protein made in a test tube with no membranes comes out longer than the

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.400
one cells secrete.

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.520
What is the extra piece, and why does the cell lose it?

4
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:19.393
The extra piece is an address, a stretch of the protein's own chain called a signal

5
00:00:19.393 --> 00:00:25.813
sequence. A ribosome builds a chain from its amino-terminal end and hides the newest

6
00:00:25.813 --> 00:00:30.413
part in a tunnel. So that end, here the address, emerges first.

7
00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:36.697
SRP binds near the ribosome's exit and reads the address as it emerges.

8
00:00:38.157 --> 00:00:43.337
In the classic preprolactin experiment, translation slowed or stopped until ER

9
00:00:43.337 --> 00:00:44.337
membranes arrived.

10
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:50.837
SRP docks on its receptor, next to Sec61, the channel into the ER.

11
00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:57.257
Like two hands gripping only while both hold GTP, they hand the ribosome to the

12
00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:58.257
channel.

13
00:00:58.617 --> 00:01:03.457
The chain begins threading in, and GTP hydrolysis opens both hands.

14
00:01:04.737 --> 00:01:07.297
The chain enters unfolded, as it is made.

15
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:13.337
Signal peptidase snips off the address: the piece the test tube kept, with no

16
00:01:13.337 --> 00:01:14.337
membrane.

17
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.280
Back to the test tube.

18
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.700
Add the membranes only after the chain is finished and released.

19
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.380
Does the protein get inside?

20
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:35.183
In this test it stays outside, and long: a released chain gives SRP no ribosome to

21
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:40.003
steer. Some ER proteins enter after release, by other routes.

22
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:42.537
So here is the idea.

23
00:01:42.537 --> 00:01:46.917
An exposed address helps specify where a protein goes.

24
00:01:47.897 --> 00:01:53.417
Where and how its reader works helps decide when it is read: as the chain grows, or

25
00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:54.417
after release.

26
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.120
A mitochondrial address also comes out first.

27
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.460
But its reader, TOM, the outer membrane's import gate, sits on the mitochondrion.

28
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.940
Most of these chains are released before TOM reads them.

29
00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.380
And a ribosome sitting on the mitochondrion?

30
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.620
Hold that question.

31
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:20.880
Released chains may start to fold, though helper proteins called chaperones often

32
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:27.920
keep them loose. To cross TOM and the inner membrane's TIM23 complex, a chain must

33
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.480
be at least partly unfolded.

34
00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:35.873
The membrane potential, a voltage across the inner membrane, pulls on the positive

35
00:02:35.873 --> 00:02:42.393
address. Inside, the chaperone Hsp70 uses ATP to grip the chain.

36
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:47.387
Put that address on a mouse enzyme, and the enzyme goes in too.

37
00:02:48.707 --> 00:02:52.707
Lock the enzyme folded with the drug methotrexate, and import stops.

38
00:02:53.947 --> 00:02:55.007
It had to unfold.

39
00:02:56.167 --> 00:03:01.447
A peroxisomal address is often the last three amino acids, such as SKL.

40
00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:07.067
They leave the tunnel only as the chain is released, so they are read afterwards.

41
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.120
Its reader, PEX5, is a receptor free in the cytosol.

42
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.020
It carries the finished protein in.

43
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.740
Unlike mitochondria, peroxisomes can import cargo that stays folded.

44
00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.640
How it crosses is still debated.

45
00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:28.753
So the address points the way, and where its reader works shapes the timing.

46
00:03:30.153 --> 00:03:34.293
SRP meets growing chains; other readers mostly meet finished ones.

47
00:03:35.753 --> 00:03:39.673
Mitochondria need those unfolded; peroxisomes can take them folded.
